QubeVM Emulator (QEMU) for Android

================================================================================

1. What is QubeVM?

QubeVM is a QEMU-based emulator for Android. It currently supports PC emulation
for multiple architectures.
For more information, instructions, guides, known issues, and downloads visit:
https://github.com/rhn-1k/QubeVM

===============================================================================

2. Requirements:

    Android SDK (platform 37.0.0 / build-tools 37.0.0)
    Android NDK r30 (clang)
    JDK 17
    Meson and Ninja (for QEMU's build system)
    Linux Desktop pc (Debian-based distro preferred)
    Make sure you have the following packages installed, if not run:
    sudo apt-get install make autoconf automake git python3 python3-pip binutils
    sudo apt-get install libtool-bin flex bison gettext texinfo rsync gtk-doc-tools ninja-build
    sudo pip3 install --upgrade meson mako
    For development you can use your own editors, Geany is highly
	recommended for editing the native code

===============================================================================

3. Known Issues:
    https://github.com/rhn-1k/QubeVM/issues

===============================================================================

4. Update Configuration

    If you build the supported QEMU version then the only thing you need to do is edit file:
    qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/android-qube-config.mak
    In all other cases you have to look at the config files under:
    qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/android-config/
    
    For more information see the Building section.
    	

5. Get and Compile external libraries

    # Make sure you're under the jni directory
    cd ./qube-android-lib/src/main/jni
    
    # Now download the source code for the external libraries and unzip them under the jni directory
    # Note: if some of these file links don't download with wget use your browser to download them

    ##### Get QEMU
    # Current versions supported by qube is 11.1.1 and 7.2.22
    # We will use 11.1.1 as an example in this guide
    wget https://download.qemu.org/qemu-11.1.1.tar.xz
    tar -xJf qemu-11.1.1.tar.xz
    mv qemu-11.1.1 qemu

    ##### GET glib
    wget https://download.gnome.org/sources/glib/2.88/glib-2.88.3.tar.xz
    tar -xJf glib-2.88.3.tar.xz
    mv glib-2.88.3 glib

    ##### GET libffi
    wget https://github.com/libffi/libffi/releases/download/v3.8.0/libffi-3.8.0.tar.gz
    tar -xzf libffi-3.8.0.tar.gz
    mv libffi-3.8.0 libffi

    ##### GET pixman
    wget https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/pixman/pixman/-/archive/pixman-0.46.4/pixman-pixman-0.46.4.tar.gz
    tar -xzf pixman-pixman-0.46.4.tar.gz
    mv pixman-pixman-0.46.4 pixman
    cd pixman && bash autogen.sh && cd ..

    ##### GET libslirp (QEMU 7.2+ need to build it separately, built as a subproject)
    wget https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/slirp/libslirp/-/archive/v4.9.4/libslirp-v4.9.4.zip
    mkdir -p qemu/subprojects
    unzip libslirp-v4.9.4.zip -d qemu/subprojects/
    mv qemu/subprojects/libslirp-v4.9.4 qemu/subprojects/libslirp

    ##### GET libepoxy (required for virgl/OpenGL)
    wget https://github.com/anholt/libepoxy/archive/refs/tags/1.5.10.zip -O libepoxy-1.5.10.zip
    unzip libepoxy-1.5.10.zip
    mv libepoxy-1.5.10 libepoxy

    ##### GET virglrenderer (required for Hardware Acceleration, tracks upstream master)
    git clone https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/virgl/virglrenderer.git virglrenderer

    Now you should have this directory structure:
    jni/
        android-config/
        compat/
        glib/
        libepoxy/
        libffi/
        qube/
        patches/
        pixman/
        qemu/
        virglrenderer/

===============================================================================

6. Apply patches

    ### Apply patch for QEMU 11.1.1:
    cd ./qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/qemu/
    patch -p1 < ../patches/qemu-11.1.1.patch

    ### Apply glib patch for QubeVM:
    cd ./qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/glib/
    patch -p1 < ../patches/glib-2.88.3.patch
    # note: if you're planning to build QEMU 7.2.22 use this instead:
    patch -p1 < ../patches/glib-2.88.3-qemu7.patch

    ### Apply libslirp patches for QubeVM:
    cd ./qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/qemu/subprojects/libslirp/
    patch -p1 < ../../../patches/libslirp-4.9.4.patch

    ### Apply virglrenderer patch for QubeVM (Hardware Acceleration):
    cd ./qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/virglrenderer/
    patch -p1 < ../patches/virglrenderer-1.3.0-android-egl.patch

    ### Other QEMU versions:
    # If you want to build QubeVM build with other QEMU versions, create your own patch like this:
    cd /qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/qemu/
    diff -ru --no-dereference /tmp/qemu-x.x.x . > ../patches/qemu-x.x.x.patch
    Don't forget to create your android-qemu-config-x.x.x.mak file
      and include it in android-qemu-config.mak

===============================================================================

7. Build

    a. To build the native part of the app make sure you're under the jni directory:
        cd qube-android-lib/src/main/jni
        
    Make sure you update file android-qube-config.mak with the NDK folder to your installation folder.
    QEMU 11.1.1 requires clang, built with Android NDK r30:
    NDK_ROOT = /home/dev/tools/ndk/android-ndk-r30

    The rest of the configuration is for specifying the right QEMU version: USE_QEMU_VERSION=11.1.1
    the host architecture (android device): BUILD_HOST
    and the emulator guest architecture: BUILD_GUEST
    
    b. From Android Studio import BOTH the Android library qube-android-lib AND the module for the guest
        architecture you need (x86,arm,ppc) ie qube-android-x86.
        
    c. Build the native libraries:
        # Make sure you're still under the jni directory:
        cd qube-android-lib/src/main/jni

        # Instead of editing android-qube-config.mak you can also supply the variables on the command line.
        # For example:
            export USE_QEMU_VERSION=11.1.1
            export BUILD_HOST=<EABI>
            export BUILD_GUEST=<GUEST_ARCH>
            export NDK_TOOLCHAIN_VERSION=clang
            export NDK_DEBUG=<ENABLE_DEBUG>

        where:
            EABI is the Android device type (host arch): armeabi-v7a, arm64-v8a, x86, x86_64
            GUEST_ARCH is the Emulator type: x86_64-softmmu,aarch64-softmmu,ppc64-softmmu
            ENABLE_DEBUG is 1 (optional)

        # Note: QEMU 11.1.1 dropped 32-bit host support. For ARM hosts, use arm64-v8a.

        # To start the build type on your terminal:
            make qube
            
        Notes:
        If you want to remove ALL previously compiled native objects and libraries:
        make clean

        If you're building apk for multiple host architectures you need to do in between builds:
        make distclean

        If you're building apk for multiple guest architectures you can specify them with commas:
        BUILD_GUEST=x86_64-softmmu,aarch64-softmmu

        Examples:
        1) To build QubeVM x86 Emulator for ARM64 phones type:
            export USE_QEMU_VERSION=11.1.1
            export BUILD_HOST=arm64-v8a
            export BUILD_GUEST=x86_64-softmmu
            make qube

        2) To build QubeVM ARM Emulator for ARM64 phones type:
            export USE_QEMU_VERSION=11.1.1
            export BUILD_HOST=arm64-v8a
            export BUILD_GUEST=aarch64-softmmu
            make qube

        3) To build QubeVM ARM Emulator for ARM64 phones for debugging type:
            export NDK_DEBUG=1
            export USE_QEMU_VERSION=11.1.1
            export BUILD_HOST=arm64-v8a
            export BUILD_GUEST=aarch64-softmmu
            make qube

        4) To build multiple QubeVM emulators for ARM64 phones type:
            export USE_QEMU_VERSION=11.1.1
            export BUILD_HOST=arm64-v8a
            export BUILD_GUEST=x86_64-softmmu,aarch64-softmmu
            make qube

		For more options see android-qube-config.mak

        Venus (Vulkan passthrough) needs USE_VENUS=true on top of virgl, on both
        the native build and the apk:
            export USE_VENUS=true
            make qube
            ./gradlew assembleRelease -PuseVenus=true

        Important:
        -PuseVenus only toggles the Venus option in the app's UI. It doesn't
        compile Venus itself, so it does nothing unless the native libs were
        also built with USE_VENUS=true.
        The Venus feature has been introduced in QEMU 9.2.2
        So you should disable Venus if you're planning to build QEMU 7.2.22
	
       You should now have the following libraries in these 2 folders:

       qube-android-lib/src/main/jniLibs/<EABI>/
         libqube.so
         libcompat-musl.so
         libcompat-qube.so
         libglib-2.0.so
         libpixman-1.so
         libepoxy.so
         libvirglrenderer.so
      qube-android-<GUEST_ARCH>/src/main/jniLibs/<EABI>/
         libqemu-system-xxx.so

        Note:
            When you build the apk in Android Studio it will contain the libraries from both
            folders so you don't need to copy files manually.

    d. Build the Android apk for the corresponding guest using Android Studio.
        Make sure the *.so libraries are zipped in the final .apk
        
    e. To build from the command line instead of Android Studio:
        export ANDROID_SDK_ROOT=~/Android/Sdk
    	gradle wrapper
        ./gradlew assembleRelease -PuseVenus=true/false

    f. If you want to build the debugging version:
        Set variables in Config.java:
          debug = true;
        Modify android-config/android-qube-config.mak and point to a configuration
          with no optimization:
        USE_OPTIMIZATION=false

        Follow the steps to build the native libraries as described above
        export NDK_DEBUG=1

        Important:
           From Android studio click Build> Rebuild Project and Run > Debug

===============================================================================

8. Debugging
        To debug the native code for a particular guest:

        # for x86 guest and ARM64 phone:
        export BUILD_HOST=arm64-v8a
        export BUILD_GUEST=x86_64-softmmu
        make ndk-gdb PKG_NAME=com.qubevm.main

        # for other guests use respectively:
        make ndk-gdb PKG_NAME=com.qubevm.arm
		make ndk-gdb PKG_NAME=com.qubevm.ppc

        Note: you might want to disable signal handling in gdb:
        handle all nostop noprint

        To catch a SIGSEGV:
        handle 11 stop print

        Useful commands:
        To set breakpoints:       b <file.c>:<code_line>
        To step statement:        s
        To go to next statement:  n
        To continue:              c
        To halt(interrupt):       ctrl-c
        To see stack (backtrace): bt

===============================================================================

9. Development Notes

    a. Codes Changes for Android compatibility are in patch files marked with __ANDROID__

    b. Similarly for QUBE functionality code changes are tagged with __QUBE__

    c. Important Configuration files (You'll need to update this with the NDK path before compiling):
        qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/android-config/android-qube-config.mak

    d. Advanced QEMU config files:
        qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/android-config/android-qemu-config.mak

    d. Advanced Device Configuration files:
        qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/android-config/android-config/*.mak

    e. Important Makefiles:
        qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/Makefile
        qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/Android.mk
        qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/Application.mk
        qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/android-qube-build.mak
        qube-android-lib/src/main/jni/android-qemu-build.mak

    f. Frontend UI options configuration see: Config.java
    
    g. When using git apply the following setting to your config file if you want to use 
        return new lines for windows:
    [core]
        eol = true
        autocrlf = input
        fileMode = false
        
===============================================================================
10. Run

    a. Start the QubeVM app and choose CPU, Memory (~8-512MB),etc..
    b. Choose a bootable disk image(s) for CDRom, Floppy, and a HDD image
    c. Start the virtual machine.
    d. For more instructions and guides visit:
        https://github.com/rhn-1k/QubeVM
    f. Have fun!

